ally the money the only  cost-effective way of doing this in a  high-throughput manner is obviously to  new to use next-generation sequencing  with  a reasonably high throughput in terms of  the number of samples that you can cover  so kyogen has actually had a  solution for sequencing the viral genome  out since early 2020 and that solution  is based it's on the right hand side of  this slide  and it's called the kaisek sars cove 2  primer panel now this  combination of of kits that we have a  primer panel as well as a library prep  ngs library preparation  kit called the kaiseka fx dna library  kit this combination of those two kits  allows for the  amplification and enrichment of  of the entire viral genome using the  arctic based approach and the arctic  based approach that i've referred to  here is comes from the arctic network a  sort of a public effort um and that team  has been involved in the past in in many  um outbreaks particularly ebola and  others that have occurred and  very quickly after the development of  the of the stars cove ii um pandemic  last year the arctic team developed a  series of protocols and primers  to enrich for the virus so that you  could sequence the whole viral genome  and uh kaijan  built um this  kyasic sars koftu primer panel based on  that arctic approach um which was  originally developed uh particularly for  compatibility with long read sequencers  like those from oxford nanopore so the  amplicons that are used to amplify the  virus in the case of the arctic approach  are about 400 base pairs long they are  overlapping so that you get redund  redundancy across the entire genome and  they give very high levels of  of sequence coverage and uniformity and  and we see that with um with uh with our  primer panel as well  they also in the case uh that we have  when you pair our primer panel with our  kaiseek fx dna library kit um you have  the ability to  uh to to multiplex samples up to 384  using  the unique dual indices that come along  with this fx dna library kit  now having said that we have seen and  we've heard from our customers um that  there are some challenges with uh with  this workflow even though it gives very  very high performance i'll show you some  of that performance data in a few slides  a little bit later in the presentation  um but  one of the challenges that a lot of labs  face with this workflow is that because  the amplicons are 400 base pairs in size  they require fragmentation if you want  to run those fragments and sequence them  on short read sequencers  it's also a workflow that involves  ligation steps in our case  with a number of beat cleanups and  quantification steps and so what we have  found is that in practice  executing this workflow for a reasonable  number of samples can can easily take a  day and a half  or or more  and  as we looked at the growing need for  speed and scale up and throughput  we realized that we needed to look again  at this workflow and see how we might be  able to optimize it in the entire kit  and optimize it and that's where  the new kit that i'm talking about today  the kaiseek direct source cove 2 kit  comes from  right this is a kit that  is a it's a single box solution for  doing both viral amplification  as well as  live ngs library preparation so it's  it's just one kit in particular we've  been able to reduce the  workflow down to about a four hour um  four or four and a half hour library  prep  workflow it requires  fewer steps dramatically less hands-on  time and fewer steps there's no  fragmentation fragmentation or ligation  involved  and that has actually allowed us to drop  the amount of plastic where needed for  this kit compared to  our arctic solution over here  and it still provides extremely highly  uniform in fact even better more highly  uniform coverage of the genome  than than even with our primer panel and  i'll show you in the next few slides uh  some of that data  a key component of this kit is that it 