 to multiplex up to  768 samples on one  sequencing run now obviously that number  of samples requires a novaseq and the  right kind of flow cell  and that additional set of udis is  coming shortly  so here's the comparison if you will  side by side of the current arctic  workflow on the bottom of the screen  here  um with the  the new shorter workflow for the sars  for the direct source cove2 kit and as  you can see with the arctic base  workflow there are  these first steps here are done with our  primer panel and then with our kaiseek  fx dna library kit to actually generate  the libraries that can be sequenced on  the sequencer i should point out that  our primer panel is also compatible with  other library prep kits from from other  vendors so you're not just um  restricted to using a kaijen kit here  for the for the ngs library prep  this workflow uh as we said uh earlier  takes uh 10 to 16 hours or so  um you know hands up not all hands on of  course but elapsed time uh when you've  got a reasonable number of samples what  we've been able to do is remove a number  of steps in that workflow and you can  see that here for the direct kit in the  upper part of the slide so you go from  viral rna sample  through cdna synthesis  direct enrichment of the viral genome  there's a beat cleanup in the middle  a quantification normalization step here  then you library  index and amplify another beam cleanup  and then your libraries are ready to go  onto the sequencer and in our hands  we can process 96 samples using manual  methods actually using multi-channel  pipetter for example in four to five  hours  we are also  recognizing that this is a streamlined  workflow that has a greater option for  automation  and we're actively working on a number  of automation scripts to automate this  on some  robotic liquid handlers there'll be more  information of that coming from kaiju  shortly  so removing these workflow challenges  it's it's a simpler uh workflow less  hands-on ti